                                    Linux Basic Commands

username@hostname:~#

username = the name of the logged in user 
hostname = the system's name
:~  = represents the current working directory
# or $ = indicate a type of user

# = root user
$ = normal user

commands in linux follow basic structure:

<command> <options> <arguments>

commmand: perform specific  tasks
options: use filters
arguments: input

1. sudo su - / sudo -i = (Super User DO) (Switch User)
--> switch to root account
syntax: sudo su -

2. man man : (manual)
--> this command is used to open manual
syntax: man man
"use Q to quit"

3. ls : (list)
--> display current directory/folder contents
i] syntax: ls

ii] syntax: ls <folder_name>
--> to display specific directory contents
eg: ls /etc

4. mkdir : (Make Directory)
--> to create directory/folder
syntax: mkdir <folder_name>
eg: mkdir demo

syntax: mkdir <folder1> <folder2> <folder3>..
--> to create multiple folders
eg: mkdir folder1 folder2 folder3

syntax: mkdir -p <parent-folder>/<child-folder>
--> to create neasted directory
eg: mkdir -p dev/test/prod

5. rmdir : (Remove Directory)
--> to delete empty directory
syntax: rmdir <folder-name>
eg: rmdir demo

6. rm -rf 
here,
rm stands for remove
-r stands for recursive
-f stands for forecefully

-r tells rm to recursively delete the directory and all its sub contents.
recursive means that the command will operate on a directory and all its contents
syntax: rm -rf <folder_name>
eg: rm -rf dev

7. touch :
--> used to create empty file.
syntax: touch <file_name>
eg: touch demo.txt

8. rm : (remove)
--> to delete file
syntax: rm <file_name>
eg: rm demo.txt

9. cat:
--> to display file contents
syntax: cat <file_name>
eg: cat demo.txt

10. cat >
--> cat command can be used to create file and add contents to it.
syntax: cat > <file_name>
eg: cat > file.txt

"Type file contents and then use ctrl + d to save and exit"

11. cat >>
--> to append contents to the file
syntax: cat >> <file_name>
eg: cat >> file.txt

12. cp : (copy)
--> used to copy files
syntax: cp <source_file> <destination>
eg: cp  file.txt  test.txt

13. cp -r:
--> used to copy directory
syntax: cp -r <dir_name> <destination_folder>
eg: cp -r test demo

14. head:
--> used to display first few (10 lines) lines of file.
syntax: head <file_name>
eg: head file.txt

15. tail:
--> used to display last few lines (10 lines) of file.
syntax: tail <file_name>
eg: tail file.txt

16. mv : (move)
1. move file
syntax: mv <soure_file> <destination>
eg: mv demo.txt folder

2. rename file
syntax:  mv <old_file_name> <new_file_name>
eg: mv  demo.txt  test.txt

17. wc: (word count)
--> it is used to find out no of lines, word count, character count 
syntax: wc <file_name>
eg: wc demo.txt

18. sort : 
--> this command is used to sort file
syntax: sort <file_name>
eg: sort demo.txt

19. uniq:
--> this command used to delete all adjecent duplicate  lines.
syntax: uniq <file_name>
eg: uniq demo.txt

20. grep:
--> the grep command is used to search for a specific word or pattern inside a file.
syntax: grep "pattern" <file_name>
eg: grep "rose" new.txt

options:
-i : to ignore case
-n : to display line numbers where the match is found

21. cd : Change Directory
--> this command is used to move between directories.
syntax: cd <file_name>
eg: cd folder

22. clear , ctrl+l : to clear screen

| : pipe
--> it is used to send the output of one command as the input to another command.
syntax: command1 | command2
eg: sort new.txt | uniq






